            %li
              %p
                One can present dependent type theory with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hierarchy+of+type+universes"} hierarchy of type universes
                such that every type is a term of a universe of the hierarchy. Examples of such dependent type theories include the one found in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book"} HoTT book
                and the proof assistants
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lean"} Lean
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cubical+Agda"} Cubical Agda
            %li
              %p
                Alternatively, one can present dependent type theory with a separate type
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
                and no
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
                at all. Examples of such dependent type theories include the one found in
                = succeed "\u2018s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+Rijke"} Egbert Rijke
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Homotopy+Type+Theory"} Introduction to Homotopy Type Theory
          %p
            In the first presentation of dependent type theory, the concept of a “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            \-small type variable” exists: since types are terms of universes, type variables are term variables where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            is extended by a free variable of a universe. Thus, the type theory is polymorphic in the sense that it has
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
              \-small polymorphism
            for all universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            in the universe hierarchy.
          %p
            In the second presentation of dependent type theory, the theory does not come with the concept of a type variable, since the context can only be extended by term judgments, and not type judgments. While this is sufficient to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+universes"} univalent universes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            in the type theory, there are a few reasons why one might want to extend dependent type theory with type variables to form polymorphic dependent type theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Large recursion principles of inductive types and higher inductive types
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                are principles where given some existing data one can construct a type family
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x)_{x:T}
                indexed by the inductive type
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to U
                into a type universe
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+booleans"} type of booleans
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+type"} circle type
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+quotients"} graph quotients
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-types"} W-types
            %li
              %p
                The concept of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
                can be implemented as applying to the entire type theory, rather than to a single universe. Impredicative polymorphism is important for:
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    defining axioms such as
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UIP"} UIP
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theoretic+axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
                    and the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                    as actual axioms rather than unjustified rules. Axioms involving type families can be represented using an auxiliary type and a function to the index type, where the type family itself is represented by the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+types"} fiber types
                    of the function.
                %li
                  %p
                    defining
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative"} impredicative
                    structures in
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+spaces"} Cauchy complete spaces
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topoi"} Grothendieck topoi
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+all+propositions"} type of all propositions
            %li
              %p
                With type variables, one can define
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = B
                between types
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    With identity types between types, it is possible to define the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
                    and thus make the type theory a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+type+theory"} univalent type theory
                    without requiring universes in the type theory. This is important since the univalence axiom implies the large recursion principles discussed in the previous point.
                %li
                  %p
                    In a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                    without
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objective+type+theory"} objective type theory
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification"} identification
                    between types to represent
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
                    or
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+conversion"} explicit conversion
                %li
                  %p
                    The same goes with the weak large recursion principles: in the absence of either judgmental equality or identity types between types, the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rules"} computation rules
                    associated with large recursion principles state that one can construct an equivalence of types between certain types given in the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rules"} elimination rules
                    of the large recursion principles. With identity types between types, one can simply make use of an
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification"} identification
                    between types.
          %p
            Polymorphic dependent type theory with type variables is thus similar to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/System+F"} System F
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polymorphism"} polymorphic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
            with type variables.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We assume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with context judgments
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \; \mathrm{type}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            and the usual context-creating rules and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            for the dependent type theory.
          %p To extend the above theory with type variables, we add the following rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{(\Gamma, A \; \mathrm{type}) \; \mathrm{ctx}}
          %p
            which states that it is possible to extend the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            by a type judgment, and the following rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, A \; \mathrm{type}, \Delta \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma, A \; \mathrm{type}, \Delta \vdash A \; \mathrm{type}}
          %p which states that we may derive a type judgment if the type judgment is already in the context.
          %p
            It is conjectured in the
            %a{:href => "#CTZulip"} Category Theory Zulip
            that the substitution rules and weakening rules for type variables are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rules"} admissible rules
          %h3#judgmental_equality Judgmental equality
          %p
            If the type theory has additional judgments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \equiv b:A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \equiv B \; \mathrm{type}
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality+of+terms"} judgmental equality of terms
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality+of+types"} judgmental equality of types
            respectively and the associated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            for judgmental equality, then the corresponding rules for judgmental equality involving type variables (i.e. substitution is a congruence, variable conversion, etc) should also be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rules"} admissible rules
          %h3#impredicative_polymorphism Impredicative polymorphism
          %p
            In the presentation of dependent type theory using a hierarchy of universes,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
            is a resizing axiom which says that for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctions"} endofunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:U_0 \to U_0
            on the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} dependent product type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 0
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{X:U_0} P(X)
            is (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+small+type"}> essentially
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+type"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
                \-small
          %p
            In the presentation of dependent type theory without universes but with a separate type judgment, while there is no universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
            to quantify over for the dependent product type, using the type variable we can add an untyped version of the dependent product type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mi X
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi X.P(X)
            to the type theory. This type is similar to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi X
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall X.P(X)
            in untyped
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mi X
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi X.P(X)
            plays the same role in this presentation of dependent type theory that the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 0
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{X:U_0} P(X)
            does for the other presentation of dependnet type theory. The rules for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mi X
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi X.P(X)
            are as follows:
          %p Formation rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Π
                      %mi X
                      %mo .
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, X \; \mathrm{type} \vdash P(X) \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash \Pi X.P(X) \; \mathrm{type}}
          %p Introduction rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi λ
                      %mi X
                      %mo .
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Π
                      %mi X
                      %mo .
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, X \; \mathrm{type} \vdash P(X) \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma, X \; \mathrm{type} \vdash p(X):P(x)}{\Gamma \vdash \lambda X.p(X):\Pi X.P(X)}
          %p Elimination rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi Π
                      %mi X
                      %mo .
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ev
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, X \; \mathrm{type} \vdash P(X) \; \mathrm{type} \quad \Gamma \vdash p:\Pi X.P(X)}{\Gamma, A \; \mathrm{type} \vdash \mathrm{ev}(p, X):P(X)}
          %p Computation rules:
          %ul
            %li
              Judgmental computation rule:
              .maruku-equation
